        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Kulikovo</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">8 September 1380 (a Julian date, not converted); the Russian law of 1995 marks the victory on 21 September</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Kulikovo Field, near the mouth of the Nepryadva where it meets the Don, now in Tula Oblast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; the exact ground is disputed</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">Russian principalities under Grand Prince Dmitry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-bold">Vladimir Andreyevich of Serpukhov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-bobrok-volynsky">Dmitry Bobrok-Volynsky</a> commanding the ambush regiment; the Horde of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a>, who was not a Genghisid and ruled through puppet khans. No source gives the numbers reliably: modern estimates for the Russians run from 6,000 or 7,000 to 36,000 and more.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Russians held the field and pursued the Horde for more than 50 kilometres. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a> fled and was killed soon after, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> took the whole Horde, and in 1382 he burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="empire-meta">7 min read</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The course of the battle rests on late tellings, above all the Tale of the Rout of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a> from the 16th century, and no source from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> side survives. Estimates of the Russian army run from 6,000 or 7,000 to hundreds of thousands in the chronicles.</li><li>A regiment hidden in an oak grove struck the Horde's flank when the Russian left and the great banner had given way, and the Horde broke and was chased more than 50 kilometres.</li><li>The victory did not end Mongol rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>, who gained most from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a>'s fall, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1382, and the Horde's overlordship was formally ended only in 1480.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The prince is said to have exchanged horses and armour with his boyar Mikhail Brenok so that he could fight as an ordinary boyar. Brenok was found dead under the great banner, and Dmitry was found among the corpses.</p></aside>
    </section>

