    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1374</span><span class="rf-text">Dmitry stops paying tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a> and open conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and Mamai begins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1378</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a>'s army under Murza Begich is defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> forces at the Vozha River</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1380</span><span class="rf-text">Dmitry gathers an army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolomna">Kolomna</a> and moves south, crossing the Oka and then the Don</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1380</span><span class="rf-text">The Russian army and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a>'s Horde fight at Kulikovo Field, opened by the duel of Peresvet and his Horde opponent</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1380</span><span class="rf-text">A regiment placed in ambush strikes the Horde and the Horde is routed and pursued to the Krasivaya Mecha</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1382</span><span class="rf-text">Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> takes and burns <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and Dmitry accepts him as sovereign</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1389</span><span class="rf-text">Dmitry leaves the grand princely throne to his son in his own will, without asking the khan</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1480</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-stand-on-the-ugra-river">Great Stand on the Ugra River</a> formally ends Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-kulikovo" data-v="13b1bc7896" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Russian regiments take their places</b> The Large regiment and the Grand Prince's court stood in the centre, with the Right Hand and Left Hand regiments on the flanks and the sentry regiment in front. The ambush regiment waited hidden in an oak grove up the Don.</li><li><b>The sentry regiment fights the Tatar vanguard</b> After small skirmishes of the advanced detachments, the sentry regiment fought the Tatar vanguard in front of the Large regiment.</li><li><b>The main forces meet in the centre</b> The Tatar force came down from one hill and stopped, with no room to spread out, while the Grand Prince's army advanced against it from the other. The fighting in the centre was long.</li><li><b>The Right Hand regiment attacks the Tatars</b> On the right Prince Andrei Olgerdovich fell on the Tatars more than once and killed many, but his men did not dare to pursue far while the Large regiment stood unmoving.</li><li><b>The Tatars break the Left Hand regiment</b> The Tatars struck mainly at the Left Hand regiment. It could not hold, broke away from the Large regiment and ran towards the Nepryadva, with the Tatars in pursuit.</li><li><b>The ambush regiment strikes the Tatar rear</b> When the Tatars broke through to the river and turned their backs to the ambush regiment, Bobrok ordered it in. Its cavalry struck the main Tatar force from behind while the regiments of Andrei and Dmitry Olgerdovich attacked.</li><li><b>The Horde flees and the ambush regiment pursues</b> The Tatars fell into confusion and ran. With no reserve to cover their retreat, the whole force left the field, and the ambush regiment chased them as far as the Krasivaya Mecha.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jani-beg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jani-beg.jpg" alt="Jani Beg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jani-beg">Jani Beg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jani_Beg_Khan_enthroned,_in_Timurid_genealogy_1405-1409_(Topkapi_Sarayi_M%C3%BCzesi,_H2152)_f.38b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1405-1409, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the Mongol invasion of Rus' in the 13th century the Russian principalities became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> grew in power and often challenged its neighbours. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a> became prince of Moscow in 1359 at the age of nine, and by 1365 had forced the princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a> to give up claims to the Grand Duchy of Vladimir. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-of-tver">Mikhail of Tver</a> received a jarliq (a khan's patent) for Vladimir from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a> in 1371 and again in 1375, but Moscow's troops kept him out, and in 1375 Dmitry forced Tver to submit. A truce with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1372 recognised Vladimir as the hereditary possession of the Moscow princes.</p><p>In the Horde, the killing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berdibeg">Berdi Beg</a> in 1359 began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-troubles">Great Troubles</a>. Mamai, his son-in-law and beylerbey, took power in the western part. He was not a Genghisid, so he enthroned khans of that line and ruled through them, always contending with rivals. Open conflict between Dmitry and Mamai began in 1374, though the exact causes are unknown; the puppet khans were by then plainly illegitimate and Mamai demanded more money as he lost the war for the Horde's throne. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> force defeated the army of Nizhny Novgorod on the Pyana in 1377, but Begich's army was defeated on the Vozha in 1378. Meanwhile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>, who had the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Tamerlane</a>, took power in the east of the Horde.</p><p>In 1380 Mamai's envoys demanded tribute as in the time of Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jani-beg">Jani Beg</a>, and Dmitry offered only the sum of his earlier agreement with Mamai. Mamai allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> of Lithuania and counted on Oleg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a>, though Oleg's part is sometimes challenged.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The earliest chronicle tales give no details of the Russian army's make-up. Gorsky, a Russian historian, reconstructed it from two chronicles he judges reliable: detachments from most of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> princes and of the upper Oka principalities, and the Lithuanian princes Andrei of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> and Dmitri of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bryansk">Bryansk</a>, but none from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a> or Tver. The lists of princes in the Tale of the Rout of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a>, he holds, cannot be trusted. Russian sources give 150,000 to 200,000 men, or 300,000 and more, and historians have reduced these steadily: 100,000 to 150,000 (Maslovsky, 1881), 50,000 to 60,000 (Razin, 1957), at most 36,000 (Kirpichnikov, 1966), 6,000 to 7,000 (Dvurechensky, 2014), and 20,000 (Kuzmin).</p><p>The Russian army was formed into a Patrol (sentry), a Forward, a Right Hand, a Left Hand, a Large and an Ambush regiment, divided into about 23 banners. One account places the Large Regiment and Dmitry's court in the centre under the okolnichy Timofey Velyaminov, the Right Hand under Andrei of Polotsk, and the Left Hand under Vasily of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslavl">Yaroslavl</a> and Fyodor of Molozha. The Ambush regiment under Vladimir Andreyevich and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-bobrok-volynsky">Dmitry Bobrok</a> stood in an oak grove; later tellings differ on which flank. Ravines guarded the flanks.</p><p>Russian sources give the Horde only as innumerable, and no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> source survives. Mamai hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, and a later source gives a Genoese infantry in his centre, which Zhukov doubts. Modern figures for the Horde run from 7,000 (Dvurechensky) to 30,000 (Kuzmin) and 100,000 or more. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> and Oleg arrived too late to take part.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dmitry gathered his army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolomna">Kolomna</a> and left on 20 August. He crossed the Oka at Lopasnya and moved south, reaching the Don on 6 September. A council decided to cross before the allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a> could join him, though this cut off the way back. On the morning of 8 September, in thick fog, the army crossed the Don; according to the Nikon Chronicle the bridges were then destroyed. It took position on the clean field by the mouth of the Nepryadva, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> appeared and formed against it.</p><p>The Tale of the Rout of Mamai, which gives the fullest course, has the champions Alexander Peresvet and Temir-Murza (also Chelubey) open the battle: each killed the other with a spear. The main forces met, by the Expanded Chronicle Tale, at the sixth hour of the day. Dmitry, in the Tale's account, stood in the ranks in a boyar's armour and left his banner with the boyar Brenok. The fighting was long and the field too narrow for the Horde to use its mobility and flanking. The Tatars slowly gained ground, threw down the great banner and killed Brenok. The Left Hand regiment was overturned and the Russian army seemed close to rout.</p><p>Then the ambush regiment attacked the Horde's flank. People and horses, tired from the long fight, could not withstand fresh troops, and the Horde's line collapsed. The Russians pursued the Tatars more than 50 kilometres to the Krasivaya Mecha River. Dmitry was found among the corpses, exhausted.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh.jpg" alt="Tokhtamysh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_between_Timur_and_Toqtamish_Khan_in_1391._Zafarnama_of_1436_(Toqtamish_detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1436 painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The losses were heavy. A third of the commanders of the 23 banners fell, among them the princes of Beloozero and Alexander Peresvet. For six days the victorious army stayed on the field among the dead. Later tellings report more than 500 boyars dead and 253,000 of the retinue, with 50,000 left; these figures are doubtful. Razin put the Russian dead at about 25,000 to 30,000, Kirpichnikov at about 800 boyars and 5,000 to 8,000 men, Urlanis at about 10,000, and Bulychev at about a third of the Russian force.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> heard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a>'s defeat he turned his army back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. People of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> land attacked groups returning from the field, plundered them and took prisoners, whose return was still an issue in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and Ryazan treaties of 1381 and 1402. Dmitry prepared to punish Ryazan, Oleg fled, and he was later restored but accepted Dmitry as his older brother and signed a peace.</p><p>Muhammad Bolak, Mamai's puppet khan, was killed in the battle. Mamai escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, raised a new army, and with no legitimate khan found his nobles going over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>. He fled again to Crimea and was killed there. Tokhtamysh thereby united the Horde after a split of twenty years. In 1382 he marched on Moscow, took and burned it and made Dmitry accept him as sovereign.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sergius-of-radonezh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sergius-of-radonezh.jpg" alt="Sergius of Radonezh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sergius-of-radonezh">Sergius of Radonezh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sergius_of_Radonezh_vita_icon_(17_c.,_Yaroslavl_museum)_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>No source from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> side survives; if any was written it was probably lost when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> burned the archives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>. Russian accounts are late or literary. The Short and Expanded Chronicle Tales are in chronicles of the 15th century, the Zadonshchina survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> from the 1470s, and the Tale of the Rout of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a>, which dates to the 16th century and survives in over a hundred copies, gives the most complete course, though it is not known whether it rests on an earlier source or is a later reconstruction. It places Metropolitan Cyprian in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a> alive in 1380 and has Dmitry meet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergius-of-radonezh">Sergius of Radonezh</a>, which is almost certainly wrong. It and the Chronicle Tale also disagree on whether Dmitry was wounded and left the field or fought to the end unhurt. The Zadonshchina adds dead princes and the 253,000 fallen, which is doubted, and the duel of the champions may be legend.</p><p>The German chroniclers Johann von Posilge and Detmar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> wrote briefly of a battle on a river they call Blue Water, probably from Lithuanian informants. Ostrowski holds that these accounts are earlier and more accurate and show that the battle took place on the Don but was less significant than claimed.</p><p>The site is disputed. Medieval sources say only a clean field beyond the Don near the mouth of the Nepryadva. Archaeologists under Dvurechensky placed it in 2005 between the Nepryadva and the Smolka, while Azbelev argues for the river's source. Finds are few, including four spearheads and a piece of mail.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Most Russian historians since the 19th century have seen the battle as a liberation from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> yoke. Others hold that it did not itself change anything fundamental but showed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> had gained a dominant position in north-eastern Rus'. Solovyov saw it as a victory that stopped another invasion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, with the meaning for eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> of the battles of the Catalaunian Plains and Poitiers for the west. Some Western historians doubt that its importance should be stressed and call <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> the real winner, a view Gorsky shares. Vernadsky wrote that, with the Horde's quarrels continuing, the battle would have brought immediate independence, but unity was restored soon after, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> could not meet the next trial after the heavy losses of 1380.</p><p>Moscow's rise went on. In 1389 Dmitry, in his will, left the grand princely throne to his son without the khan's patent, and the Horde had to accept the new grand prince. Payment of tribute became irregular. Dmitry took the name Donskoy after the battle, though by another view he received it only under Ivan the Terrible.</p><p>The battle produced the Kulikovo cycle of Russian literature, in which later versions grew more religious. A column was set up on the field in 1852, the 500th anniversary was held there in 1880, and a state museum-reserve was founded in 1996. A law of 1995 fixes 21 September as a day of military glory; this does not match the accepted date of 8 September.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Kulikovo Field near the Don on 8 September 1380 the Russian forces of Dmitry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> defeated the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, with an ambush regiment turning the fight. Numbers and course rest on late tellings, and Mongol overlordship continued until 1480.</em></p>

